# Census 2011 and the Demography of India

> Every SSC, RRB and state-police paper carries a few questions straight out
> of the 2011 Census: the population total, the sex ratio, the literacy rate,
> the most populous state. The numbers are fixed, so the marks are easy once
> you have a clean table in your head. This lesson keeps only Census 2011
> figures, and flags the pairs that examiners love to swap.

## 1. What the Census Is and How It Is Run

[definition]
The **Census** is the decennial headcount of every person in India, carried out
by the **Registrar General and Census Commissioner of India** under the Ministry
of Home Affairs and the **Census Act, 1948**. Census 2011 was the **15th**
census since the first in 1872, and the **7th** since independence.
[/definition]

- The first census (1872) was taken region by region, in different years.
  The **first synchronous census** was taken in **1881** under Viceroy
  **Lord Ripon**, and counts have followed every ten years since.
- Census 2011 ran in two phases: house listing (April to September 2010) and
  population enumeration (9 to 28 February 2011, with the reference date of
  1 March 2011).
- The Census is on the **Union List** of the Constitution.
- Remember the units: a person aged **0 to 6** is a "child" for the
  child sex ratio, and literacy is counted for those aged **7 and above**.
- **Literate** means a person aged 7 or more who can read and write with
  understanding in any language.

## 2. Headline Figures of Census 2011

| Indicator | Census 2011 figure |
| --- | --- |
| Total population | 1,210,854,977 (about 1.21 billion) |
| Males | 623,270,258 |
| Females | 587,584,719 |
| Decadal growth, 2001 to 2011 | about 17.7 percent |
| Sex ratio | **943** females per 1,000 males |
| Child sex ratio (0 to 6 years) | **919** |
| Density of population | **382** persons per sq km |
| Literacy rate | **74.04** percent |
| Male literacy | 82.14 percent |
| Female literacy | 65.46 percent |
| Rural share | 68.84 percent |
| Urban share | about 31.2 percent |
| Districts | 640 |

- The decadal growth rate fell from about 21.5 percent in 1991 to 2001 to about
  17.7 percent in 2001 to 2011, a clear slowdown.
- The male-female literacy gap is about **16.7 percentage points** (82.14
  minus 65.46).
- **Sex ratio** is expressed per **1,000 males**.

[tip]
Memory trick: 943 is the sex ratio, 919 the child sex ratio, and the two are
"nine-four-three and nine-one-nine". Density is 382. Literacy is 74.04.
[/tip]

## 3. States at the Extremes

| Category | State | Figure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Most populous state | Uttar Pradesh | 199,812,341 |
| Second | Maharashtra | 112,374,333 |
| Third | Bihar | 104,099,452 |
| Fourth | West Bengal | 91,276,115 |
| Fifth | Madhya Pradesh | 72,626,809 |
| Least populous state | Sikkim | 610,577 |
| Least populous UT | Lakshadweep | 64,473 |
| Highest density (state) | Bihar | 1,102 per sq km |
| Lowest density (state) | Arunachal Pradesh | 17 per sq km |
| Highest density (UT) | Delhi | 11,297 per sq km |
| Highest sex ratio (state) | Kerala | 1,084 |
| Lowest sex ratio (state) | Haryana | 879 |
| Highest literacy (state) | Kerala | 94.00 percent |
| Lowest literacy (state) | Bihar | 61.80 percent |

- **Uttar Pradesh** alone holds roughly one in six Indians, and its population
  is bigger than that of most countries.
- **Nagaland** is the only state that showed a **negative** decadal growth
  rate in 2011.
- Among Union Territories the highest sex ratio belongs to **Puducherry**
  (1,037) and the lowest to **Daman and Diu** (618), as counted in 2011.
- The Union Territory of **Delhi** has density far above any state, which is
  why exam questions always say "among the states".

## 4. Religion, Caste Groups and Urbanisation

| Religion | Share of population, 2011 |
| --- | --- |
| Hindus | 79.8 percent |
| Muslims | 14.2 percent |
| Christians | 2.3 percent |
| Sikhs | 1.7 percent |
| Buddhists | 0.7 percent |
| Jains | 0.4 percent |

- **Urban areas** in the Census are of two kinds: **statutory towns**
  (declared by law, with a municipality or similar body) and **census towns**
  (population of at least 5,000, at least 75 percent of male main workers in
  non-farm work, and density of at least 400 per sq km).
- **Rural share** of 68.84 percent means about seven Indians in ten still live
  in villages.
- The country is described as having **28 states and 7 Union Territories** in
  2011. Telangana was formed in 2014, so Census 2011 counts it inside Andhra
  Pradesh.

## 5. Common Traps and a Worked Example

- **Population versus growth**: Uttar Pradesh is the most populous state, but
  Meghalaya had the highest decadal growth rate among states (about 28
  percent). Do not confuse size with rate.
- **Sex ratio versus child sex ratio**: 943 is the all-age figure; 919 is for
  ages 0 to 6.
- **Kerala has both the top sex ratio and the top literacy**, while **Bihar**
  has the lowest literacy and the highest density among states.
- **Haryana** has the lowest sex ratio of any state, a favourite for Haryana
  exams (HSSC CET, Haryana Police).
- **Provisional versus final**: provisional totals were released in 2011 and
  the final figures differ slightly. Use the final tables for exams.

[example]
Question: "If the literacy of males is 82.14 percent and of females 65.46
percent, by how many percentage points does male literacy exceed female?"
Subtract: 82.14 minus 65.46 equals 16.68 points. The overall rate (74.04)
always lies between the two, closer to the male figure because there are more
males than females.
[/example]

[summary]
Census 2011 counted about 1.21 billion Indians: 943 women per 1,000 men,
child sex ratio 919, density 382 per sq km and literacy 74.04 percent
(males 82.14, females 65.46). Uttar Pradesh is the biggest state and Sikkim the
smallest. Bihar has the highest density and the lowest literacy among states,
Kerala leads in both sex ratio (1,084) and literacy (94.00), and Haryana has
the lowest sex ratio (879). Arunachal Pradesh is the thinnest in density (17),
and Delhi, a Union Territory, the densest (11,297). About 68.84 percent of
Indians lived in rural areas.
[/summary]
